/// Short, similar-difficulty passages for the font speed test (~110 words each).
enum FontPassages {
    static let all: [String] = [
        "Before satellites and radio, lighthouses were the main way sailors knew where land began. Each one had its own pattern of flashes, so a captain who saw two quick flashes and a long pause could check a chart and know which stretch of coast lay ahead. Keepers lived in the towers for months at a time. They trimmed wicks, polished lenses, and wound the clockwork that turned the light. On stormy nights they stayed awake until morning. Today most lighthouses run on their own, powered by small lamps and solar panels, but many are still kept lit because ships use them as a backup when other systems fail.",
        "A honeybee colony works like a single animal made of thousands of parts. Young bees stay inside, cleaning cells and feeding the larvae. As they get older, they move on to building wax, guarding the entrance, and finally flying out to collect nectar. When a forager finds a good patch of flowers, she returns and performs a short dance on the comb. The angle of the dance points toward the flowers, and its length tells the others how far away they are. Within minutes, dozens of bees can head straight to a field they have never seen. The whole system runs without a leader giving any orders.",
        "Bread is made from only a few simple things: flour, water, salt, and yeast. When flour gets wet, two of its proteins join together and form gluten, a stretchy web that can trap gas. The yeast eats small amounts of sugar in the dough and gives off bubbles of carbon dioxide. Those bubbles get caught in the gluten, and the dough slowly rises. Kneading makes the web stronger, while a long, cool rest gives the yeast time to build more flavor. In the oven, the bubbles grow quickly, the outside turns brown, and the inside sets into the soft crumb that most people know and love.",
        "Twice a day, the sea along most coasts rises and falls. This happens mainly because the moon's gravity pulls on the oceans, drawing the water on the near side of the earth slightly toward it. A second bulge forms on the far side, where the pull is weakest. As the earth turns, each stretch of coast passes through both bulges, giving two high tides and two low tides. The sun has an effect too. When the sun and moon line up, the tides are larger than usual. When they pull at right angles to each other, the difference between high and low water is much smaller.",
        "Every flat map of the world is wrong in some way. The earth is round, and there is no way to spread the surface of a ball onto paper without stretching or tearing it. Mapmakers have to choose what to protect. Some maps keep the shapes of countries correct but make places near the poles look far too big, which is why Greenland can appear as large as Africa. Other maps keep the areas right but squash the shapes. Neither choice is a mistake. Each one is useful for a different job, whether that job is sailing a ship or comparing the size of two regions.",
        "In the early days of railways, every town kept its own time based on the position of the sun. Noon in one city could be several minutes different from noon in the next. This was fine when travel was slow, but train timetables became a mess, and the confusion led to missed connections and even accidents. Railway companies began using a single standard time along their lines, and countries later divided the world into time zones. The clock on your phone, which changes on its own when you fly somewhere new, is a direct result of a problem first faced by railway workers.",
    ]
}
